  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 355 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe next Pacific trough will approach the area Thursday night. This \nsystem has trended towards a slow-moving, disorganized open wave \nwith generally good agreement amongst ensemble members. Saturated \nsouthwest flow will push into Carbon County Thursday evening, \ninitiating another round of snow shower activity for the mountains. \nThe primary lifting mechanism will be orographic, but models \nactually show a decent amount of convective instability west of the \nLaramie Range Thursday night and Friday. So, accumulation is most \nlikely where the southwest orographic lift is most favorable (the \nSierra Madre Range), but we will need to watch for locally heavy \nsnow shower activity, potentially even bordering on snow squall \ncriteria, spreading out of the mountains. Snowfall rates could be \nhigh at times due to the instability, but coverage is expected to be \nspotty outside of the mountains. The higher peaks are favored to see \naccumulating snow, with a 80+% chance for Advisory criteria amounts \nin the Sierra Madre range, and a 40% chance in the Snowy Range. \nThursday night and Friday, precipitation is expected to be confined \nto areas along and west of the Laramie Range. The surface cold front \nwill be slow to move into the area, which should allow much of the \nHigh Plains to see another mild day Friday, even while Carbon, \nAlbany, and Converse counties turn colder. Once the surface front \npushes in from the northwest, sometime Friday late afternoon or \nevening, we will see north to northwest winds at the surface, which \nwill be more favorable orographic lift for the High Plains. Snow \nfalling east of the Laramie Range Friday night into Saturday morning \nis still the less likely solution, but probable enough to warrant a \nmention. \n\nThis storm system will be driven by a slow moving longwave \ntrough remaining to our north, and several vort-maxes rotating \naround it between Thursday night and Sunday. This will keep \nunsettled and chillier weather over the area through the weekend. \nLook for highs below seasonal averages, breezy conditions, and \npossibly a few snow showers though expectations for QPF are limited. \nThe flow aloft will transition to a more zonal pattern by early next \nweek. However, models are in pretty good agreement keeping a chilly \nhigh pressure system over the northern plains pushed up against the \nmountains, which should keep temperatures on the chilly side for \nseveral more days. The overall weather pattern should remain active \nwith additional Pacific troughs moving inland over the area, but \nalmost all ensemble members continue to bring these storms through \non an unfavorable track, limiting snowfall chances.",
